# Implementation quality

- Find and fix root causes rather than suppressing symptoms.
- Preserve correctness, determinism, transaction safety, lockfile compatibility, and supported-platform coverage.
- Do not weaken behavior merely to make tests pass.
- Do not silently guess when input is malformed, ambiguous, dangling, conflicting, or unsupported.
- Fail closed with typed, actionable errors.
- Propagate errors instead of swallowing them or substituting guessed fallback values.
- Never silently drop graph nodes, dependency edges, metadata, configuration, or semantic information.
- Preserve existing public behavior unless the task explicitly changes the contract.
- Prefer deterministic output and stable ordering.
- Avoid indefinite duplicate or parallel implementations.
- Remove superseded custom code when a new implementation becomes authoritative, unless a compatibility fallback or staged rollout is genuinely required.
- Add focused regression tests for every repaired defect.
- Add integration tests for behavior that crosses package, filesystem, process, transaction, or external-tool boundaries.
- Re-read affected code after implementation and review it independently from the test expectations.
- Do not trust an existing self-score or completion claim without checking the code and evidence.
